{"metric":"curzon_chazy","source":[{"metric":"curzon_chazy","system":"spherical","fields":["coords","inverse_metric_components","kretschmann","metric_components","parameters"],"version":"9aa573dff19fb178"},{"metric":"curzon_chazy","system":"weyl","fields":["coords","inverse_metric_components","kretschmann","metric_components","parameters"],"version":"5fc9afaefb27e80d"}],"views":[{"id":"weyl_axis","label":"The axis","box":[-3.4916,3.4916,-3.3916,3.3916],"system":"weyl","restriction":"The half of the axis $\\rho = 0$ with $z > 0$ only, totally geodesic, each point in the diagram a single event.","settings":"$m = GM/c^2 = 1$, the unit of every length, and $\\ell = 4\\,m$.","caption":["The half axis $\\rho = 0$, $z > 0$ of the Curzon-Chazy particle ($m = 1$), each point in the diagram a single event. The metric on it is $-e^{-2m/z}c^2dt^2 + e^{2m/z}dz^2$, and with $z_* = z\\,e^{2m/z} - 2m\\,\\mathrm{Ei}(2m/z)$, which runs from $-\\infty$ at $z = 0$ to $\\infty$, the maps $p = \\arctan((ct - z_*)/\\ell)$ and $q = \\arctan((ct + z_*)/\\ell)$ bring it into the whole diamond, drawn with $T = p + q$ up and $X = q - p$ across.","Since $g_{tt}g_{zz} = -1$, $z$ is an affine parameter along every light ray, so a ray reaches $z = 0$, the two edges on the left, after a finite affine distance, at $t \\to \\pm\\infty$. The Kretschmann scalar on the axis, $48m^2(z - m)^2e^{-4m/z}/z^8$, goes to zero there."],"layers":[{"kind":"fill","class":"region","points":[[3.1416,0.0],[0.0,3.1416],[-3.1416,0.0],[0.0,-3.1416]]},{"kind":"fill","class":"cover","points":[[3.1416,0.0],[0.0,3.1416],[-3.1416,0.0],[0.0,-3.1416]]},{"kind":"line","class":"r","points":[[-0.0,-3.1396],[-0.0045,-3.0867],[-0.0179,-3.0306],[-0.0408,-2.9712],[-0.0701,-2.9136],[-0.1135,-2.8428],[-0.1589,-2.7776],[-0.2997,-2.5996],[-0.4461,-2.4308],[-0.6817,-2.1722],[-1.8886,-0.8891],[-2.0969,-0.6578],[-2.2725,-0.449],[-2.3752,-0.3101],[-2.4094,-0.2566],[-2.4428,-0.1967],[-2.4656,-0.1464],[-2.4826,-0.0964],[-2.4928,-0.0482],[-2.496